{"step_index":2718,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-27T13:00:15Z","input_tokens":2911,"cache_read_tokens":194524,"output_tokens":4405,"thinking":"A \"Progress Management\" button, ideally near the sub-navigation, offers quick access to storage details. This button will open a modal displaying local storage statistics including the storage engine, and counts for solved, attempted, and bookmarked problems.\n\n\nThe current stage and last problem are noted for context. Progress can be exported to and imported from JSON backups, including options to merge or replace existing data. Learning preferences, such as page size and auto-reveal hints, are adjustable and saved locally.\n\n\nExamining the data that needs to be persisted locally to ensure user progress survives browser sessions. Identified problem statuses (solved, attempted, etc.), personal notes, practice history, roadmap progress, learning settings, last opened problem, and user code cache as key data points. Investigating existing storage functions and their current implementation.\n\n\nDefined storage keys for progress, settings, and user code, also for the active stage and the schema version. The aim is to create a comprehensive JSON schema to represent all user learning data. This structure will include problem statistics, detailed progress tracking, roadmap completion status, user settings, and code snippets. The data schema will support both export and import functions.\n\n\nDefining a validation engine to ensure data integrity during import. The engine will check for a valid object or parse a JSON string, then validate the schema, accepting either a top-level \"progress\" object or individual top-level progress elements.\n\nThe validation includes filtering and sanitization: \"solved,\" \"attempted,\" \"review,\" and \"bookmarked\" arrays are checked for positive integer values and duplicates removed. \"Notes\" are validated as an object with string values, and keys are parsed as positive integers, while invalid notes are rejected. History must be an array of objects that include the problemId and a timestamp.\n\n\nDeveloping validation logic for the import function. This includes rigorous checks for data type correctness and value ranges across all fields. Invalid data will be rejected, and the existing data store will remain untouched to prevent data corruption. Two import strategies, \"merge\" and \"replace,\" will be implemented with distinct data handling approaches.\n\n\nConsidering IndexedDB as a backup for local storage, especially for scenarios where data persistence beyond basic cache clearing is crucial. This will involve an asynchronous implementation alongside synchronous storage. Defining a function to save the data to IndexedDB, ensuring the data is backed up.\n\n\nLocal storage will be used for immediate progress updates, while IndexedDB will serve as an asynchronous backup solution for larger data and persistence beyond browser sessions.  The implementation will include export/import functionality to facilitate data transfer between environments, along with validation checks to ensure data integrity during import. UI will include a prominent storage/settings button in the header and a storage status indicator on the dashboard.\n\n\nUI elements are being designed for progress management, including buttons for export and import, and a modal displaying storage statistics and educational notices. The modal will present information on solved/attempted problems, notes, and roadmap progress, as well as an option to backup/restore from JSON. Both merge and replace options for import are considered.\n\n\nImplementing a validation preview card for the data import function. This includes on-the-fly JSON analysis to display a summary of detected data points, indicating the validity and completeness of the backup. An action button will be activated only upon successful validation.\n\nUser learning preferences such as page size, sort order, code editor font size, and visualizer animation speed will be added. These preferences are saved locally and are loaded on application start or reload. The \"lastOpenedProblemId\" will also be persisted across sessions.\n\nAdded a \"Danger Zone\" feature with an option to reset all user progress, requiring double confirmation to prevent accidental data loss. This also will include saving and loading user setting as well as the 'lastOpenedProblemId' information.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"view_file","args":{"AbsolutePath":"C:/Users/CVT/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/code-flow-visualizer/app_build/dsa_dashboard.js","EndLine":540,"StartLine":470,"toolAction":"Viewing loadProgress and saveProgress in dsa_dashboard.js","toolSummary":"View loadProgress and saveProgress in dsa_dashboard.js"}}]}
